        "description": "<p class=\"is-style-drop-cap-with-border wp-block-paragraph\">Part of the art and science of astrophotography is figuring out when certain flying objects in the sky are going to line up for a photo opportunity.&#160;</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">And few photographs demonstrate it better than <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/aj.smadi/?hl=en\">AJ Smadi</a>\u2019s shot of the International Space Station (ISS) hurtling in front of Jupiter. There\u2019s an incredible symmetry in the dimensions here: Jupiter is about 1.3 million times bigger than the ISS, but it\u2019s also around 1.3 million times further away.&#160;</p>\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"768\" height=\"1024\" src=\"https://lede-admin.nautil.us/wp-content/uploads/sites/70/2026/11/01-jupiter.jpg?w=710\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-1286408\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Jupiter and the ISS. <em>Credit: AJ Smadi</em></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://nautil.us/this-photo-of-the-iss-next-to-jupiter-wa",
